nts`. The two payments services I found are both unhealthy, with the same 18% error rate in all three regions (us-east, us-west, eu-west):\n\n| Service | Current version | Status | Most recent healthy release |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `payments-api` | v5.1.0 | degraded | v5.0.0 |\n| `payments-worker` | v3.4.0 | unhealthy | v3.3.0 |\n\n- Both services' newest release is bad, and both have a healthy release right before it. The deploy history has no timestamps, so I can't tell which one was this morning's deploy.\n- Both having exactly the same error rate suggests one may be causing the other's errors, but I can't tell which from here.\n- There are no open incidents yet.\n\n**What I'd do once you confirm**\n- **Option A, one service:** Roll back the one you name (`payments-api` to v5.0.0 or `payments-worker` to v3.3.0) and open a SEV2 for it.\n- **Option B, both services:** Roll back both to their last healthy versions and open one SEV2 that covers both.\n\nEither way, the rollback only goes to versions that were already healthy in production, so it doesn't need change-manager approval.\n\nWhich service was in this morning's deploy, or should I roll back both?", "type": "text"}]}, {"stop_reason": "tool_use", "content": [{"id": "toolu_01FGUHj73Nt3dKjeUXsLASC2", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {"service": "payments-worker", "version": "v3.3.0"}, "name": "rollback", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}, {"id": "toolu_01UTbtvSixvLt3NjsAsrB1bX", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {"title": "payments-worker v3.4.0 causing elevated errors; rolled back to v3.3.0", "severity": "SEV2", "service": "payments-worker", "summary": "After this morning's deploy of payments-worker v3.4.0, the service became unhealthy with an 18% error rate in us-east, us-west and eu-west. payments-api (v5.1.0) shows the same 18% error rate; per the service owner this is downstream of the worker. Action taken: rolled payments-worker back to v3.3.0, its last healthy production release, in all regions. Follow up: confirm the worker and payments-api recover, and find the root cause in v3.4.0.", "idempotency_key": "payments-worker-v3.4.0-rollback-sev2"}, "name": "create_incident", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}]}, {"stop_reason": "tool_use", "content": [{"id": "toolu_019L4dnjEJ5iK4EpbyKrVKGs", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {"service": "payments-worker"}, "name": "get_service_status", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}, {"id": "toolu_01RwcYStk1rjHXRorLcFdVof", "caller": {"type": "direct"}, "input": {"service": "payments-api"}, "name": "get_service_status", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}]}, {"stop_reason": "end_turn", "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "", "type": "thinking"}, {"citations": null, "text": "I rolled `payments-worker` back to v3.3.0 and opened SEV2 incident INC-0001. The worker has recovered, but `payments-api` is still failing at 18%.\n\n**What's done**\n- **Rollback:** `payments-worker` is now on v3.3.0, its last healthy release, in us-east, us-west and eu-west. It reports healthy, and its error rate is down from 18% to 0.2%. Since v3.3.0 was already healthy in production, no change-manager approval was needed.\n- **Incident:** INC-0001 is open and the on-call engineer has been paged. It covers the bad v3.4.0 release, the 18% errors in all regions, the effect on `payments-api` and the rollback.\n\n**Still broken: `payments-api`**\nOn my one check right after the rollback, `payments-api` was still unhealthy at 18% errors, on v5.1.0. That could just mean it hasn't caught up with the worker fix yet. But v5.1.0 is also marked \"degraded\" in its own deploy history, so it may have a separate problem.\n\n**Next step**\nCheck `payments-api` again in a few minutes. If it's still at 18%, its last healthy release is v5.0.0. I won't roll it back unless you tell me to, since you said its errors come from the worker.", "type": "text"}]}]}